  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 136 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nSatellite shows the upper level low over northeast Utah, with a\ncouple shortwaves rotating around it. The most impressive one was\nnow lifting quickly northward through central and eastern New\nMexico on the heels of an 80 kt upper level speed max. Other \nshowers and thunderstorms were developing in the slightly unstable\nand still moist airmass over western and central Colorado ahead \nof the upper level trough. It appears both of those features will\ngenerate the highest coverage of showers at least early tonight \nas there is ascent associated with each. That supports the \neastern plains and most of the mountains, while the I-25 Corridor \nwould see lower but still scattered coverage of mainly light \nshowers. \n\nLater tonight into early Monday morning, another speed max is\nforecast to eject from the base of the trough as it lifts into \nthe Central and Northern High Plains. By the time that shortwave \norganizes again, the main precipitation shield is expected to be\nmostly over the northern and far eastern plains. We still have\nabout a 30-50% chance of seeing a half inch of rain over the \nnorthern tier of counties, with those odds decreasing rather \nquickly to the south including most of the I-70 Corridor and\nDenver metro. In fact, our deterministic forecast (essentially \n50th percentile) has less than a tenth of an inch for Denver metro\narea and only about a 50% chance of a measurable rain. For the \nmountains, we still think the northern tier of mountains (e.g. \nPark/northern Gore/Medicine Bow Ranges and Rocky Mountain National\nPark) would see a 1-4\" snow, while the rest of the mountains \nincluding the I-70 Corridor through Summit County would see a \ndusting to 2\". Brief heavier showers and colder temperatures \novernight would allow some travel impacts on some of the higher \npasses, but overall a pretty light snow event. \n\nPrecipitation will quickly end behind the trough passage early\nMonday morning, with some sunshine returning. However, it may take\nsome time to clear as the plains will be shrouded in low clouds \nand areas of fog in the morning, and mixing will be slow given \nweak boundary layer winds and stronger inversions. Thus, another \ncool day with temperatures generally at or slightly below seasonal\nnormals is on tap for Monday. \n\nFor Tuesday, a stronger zonal flow will develop, leading to dry\nweather, more sunshine, and warmer temperatures due to increasing\ndownslope. Breezy conditions will develop over the mountains and\nhigh valleys with moderate pressure gradients in place. Similar\nconditions can be expected Wednesday and Thursday, with\ntemperatures warming a couple more degrees and highs pushing near\n70F on the plains and I-25 Corridor.  \n\nThere is still considerable uncertainty in next weekend's weather\nas seen in the ensemble output. There are indications a\nsubtropical push could occur as early as Friday but the main\nimpacts of this first wave would likely stay southeast of our \nforecast area. A larger trough along with a possible pineapple \nconnection is forecast to move into the southwest U.S. next \nweekend, but models are all over the place regarding it's eventual\ntrack and evolution. At this point, it still looks like areas \nover southern and western Colorado would be most prone for more \nsignificant precipitation.",
